        {
            "id": "23687745",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "02:04, 9 December 2017",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(14,931 bytes)",
            "change": 265,
            "summary": "(Replacing old test with “In a Hurry?” experiment for wikiHaus.)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "reverted_by": "GeekySonic",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Sugar rockets are a type of projectile made by combining sugar and potassium nitrate. Though they may sound innocuous, sugar rockets are powerful and can produce reactions strong enough to lift them hundreds of meters in the air.",
                    "== In a Hurry? ==",
                    "To start building a sugar rocket, cut out a section of {{convert|13|mm|in|abbr=on}} PVC pipe and place smaller, shorter pipes in each end. Grind up clay cat litter and pack it into your rocket, filling it about ⅓ of the way up. To make the propellant, grind potassium nitrate into a fine powder using a new coffee grinder or mortar. Wearing safety equipment, place your potassium nitrate and powdered sugar into a saucepan you won’t use for anything else. Heat the pan to {{convert|380|F|C}} for 20-30 minutes, stirring frequently. To learn how to prepare your rocket for lift off, read on!"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "Sugar rockets sound innocuous, but they produce thrust that can lift them hundreds of meters in the air. This wikiHow will show you how to make them.",
                    "== 10 Second Summary ==",
                    "1. Cut a short piece of PVC piping and add a retaining ring to each side.<br>",
                    "2. Pack the PVC piping with ground cat litter.<br>",
                    "3. Heat ground potassium nitrate with powdered sugar in a saucepan, stirring frequently.<br>",
                    "4. Pack it into the rocket and add more cat litter.<br>",
                    "5. Drill a hole through the litter and attach a fuse.<br>",
                    "6. Attach a stick to the side, set it in the ground, and light it."
                ]
            }
        },
