            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Cut short sections of PVC piping. Buy roughly 13 mm (½ inch) diameter PVC piping from a home improvement store. [[Cut-PVC-Pipe|Cut the pipe]] into sections, as long as you'd like your rockets to be. 7.5–10 cm (3–4 in) rockets are a good place to start.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 1 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Add a retaining ring to each side. Find a smaller PVC pipe, that just barely fits into your rocket sections. Cut this into short sections, about 6–12 mm (¼–½ in) long. Cut through each section — this allows you to expand the ring outward for a tighter fit. Apply PVC cement to the inner edge of the larger pipe, at one end. Place the smaller pipe inside the larger one, pressing it outward to form a tight fit. Repeat on the opposite end with a second retaining ring. Clamp and wait for the cement to cure, according to label instructions.<ref>http://www.jacobsrocketry.com/aer/casings_nozzles_headers.htm</ref>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 2 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Grind cat litter. Purchase unscented clay cat litter from a pet store. Keeping it dry, grind it in a coffee grinder or mortar until it becomes fine dust.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 3 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Pack the litter into each rocket. Stand each pipe on its end on a stable surface. Fill each one about ⅓ full with the cat litter dust. Tamp the litter vigorously with a wooden dowel or spool that fits snugly into the pipe. This should compress the litter into a hard, clay cap.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 4 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Purchase powdered sugar. Sugar provides the energy that will give thrust to the rocket once burned. Check the ingredients list before buying: most powdered sugar contains a little corn starch, but this will not significantly affect the rocket.<ref>http://www.nakka-rocketry.net/sucrose.html</ref> If it contains any other additives, look for a different brand.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 5 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find potassium nitrate. This chemical, KNO<sub>3</sub>, will provide oxygen to allow for fast, prolonged combustion. Buy this as \"stump killer\" at a garden supply store or home improvement store. Some brands of stump killer contain other ingredients, so check the label to make sure it is 100% KNO<sub>3</sub>.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 6 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Grind the potassium nitrate to a powder. Buy a new coffee grinder and label it \"potassium nitrate.\" Place it on a cleared counter, far away from all sugar and combustible materials. Fill halfway with potassium nitrate and grind for about 40 seconds, rotating the grinder to make sure all the powder hits the blades.<ref>http://www.nakka-rocketry.net/sucrose.html</ref> The finer the powder, the more evenly it will mix with the sugar.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 7 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find an appropriate work area. Once made, the rockets are at risk of combustion if they come into contact with heat, a spark from a metal object, or a flame. Ideally, you should make the rockets close to the area you plan to set them off. Choose an open area with no passersby. Even when intentionally set off, these rockets can damage surroundings or people when they return to earth.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 8 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Set up an electric heating station. Soon, you'll be mixing the two ingredients together over heat. There's always the risk of explosion or fire during this process. Reduce the chance of injury with the following setup:[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 9 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Wear safety equipment. There's a significant chance that the propellant mixture catches fire and explodes violently. Wear gloves, a face shield, and thick clothing that covers all exposed skin. Do not wear any clothing made from synthetic materials, which can melt onto your skin.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 10 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Add ingredients to a heat-safe container. [[Use-a-Scale|Using a kitchen scale]], measure 65 grams (2.3 ounces) powdered potassium nitrate, and bring it to your heating station. Bring the kitchen scale to the powdered sugar. Measure 35 grams (1.2 ounces) in a new container and bring that to your station. Pour both ingredients into a saucepan or frying pan that you don't plan on using for any other purpose.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 11 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Mix with baking soda (optional). This will slow down the firing, which decreases thrust but reduces risk of exploding the rocket prematurely. Mix 15g (0.5 oz) baking soda into your 100g (3.5 oz) of propellant. Use a wooden or silicone implement to stir.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 12 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Heat while stirring frequently. Place the container of sugar and potassium nitrate on the electric heat source. Heat at 193ºC (380ºF), staying as near to this temperature as you can the whole time.<ref>http://www.nakka-rocketry.net/sucrose.html</ref> Using a silicone spatula (never a metal one), stir gently to combine the two ingredients and distribute the heat. Failure to stir frequently can cause an explosion. Stir over heat until the whole mixture turns into a thick, light brown paste, similar to peanut butter. This can take up to an hour, but a batch this size will usually be ready in 20–30 minutes.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 13 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Pack the propellant into the rocket body. As soon as the hot propellant mixture is ready, pour a little into one of your prepared rockets. Immediately tamp it down with a snug-fitting dowel, making sure there are no air bubbles. Pour more propellant and tamp down until there's roughly 2.5 cm (1 inch) of space left in the tube.<ref>ftp://ftp.demec.ufpr.br/foguete/bibliografia/ref_roc4_Easy_Pvc_Rockets_Smiley.pdf</ref>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 14 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Tamp down additional cat litter. Create a second clay cap on top of the propellant, just as you did the first. Tamp down firmly to create a hard, non-crumbling cap. This should extend beneath the retaining ring and lie even with the end of the rocket.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 15 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Drill carefully through the top cap. It's time to turn the cap you just made into a nozzle, which will create thrust by expelling high-pressure exhaust. It is possible to ignite the rocket by drilling, so use extreme caution. In your fire-safe work area, drill the nozzle as follows:[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 16 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Create a core (optional). After you've penetrated the cap, you may wish to create a hollow core in the propellant. This will increase thrust by providing more surface area to combust.<ref>http://exploration.grc.nasa.gov/education/rocket/TRCRocket/practical_rocketry.html</ref> Push a dowel or aluminum rod into the propellant, pushing about halfway along the rocket's length.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 17 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Attach a fuse. Thread a cannon fuse into the hole you just drilled. Leave plenty of fuse outside the rocket for safety.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 18 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Attach a stick to the side. Tape or glue a long, sturdy, wooden skewer to the outside of the rocket. Attach it near the nozzle, with most of the skewer extending beyond it.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 19 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Set in the ground and light. Plant the stick firmly into the ground so the rocket points upward. Confirm that the stick and rocket are stable. Warn everyone in the area. Light the fuse and step well back. Congratulations! You've launched your first sugar rocket.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 20 Version 3.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Cut short sections of PVC piping. Buy roughly 13 mm (½ inch) diameter PVC piping from a home improvement store. [[Cut-PVC-Pipe|Cut the pipe]] into sections, as long as you'd like your rockets to be. 7.5–10 cm (3–4 in) rockets are a good place to start.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 1 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Add a retaining ring to each side. Find a smaller PVC pipe, that just barely fits into your rocket sections. Cut this into short sections, about 6–12 mm (¼–½ in) long. Cut through each section — this allows you to expand the ring outward for a tighter fit. Apply PVC cement to the inner edge of the larger pipe, at one end. Place the smaller pipe inside the larger one, pressing it outward to form a tight fit. Repeat on the opposite end with a second retaining ring. Clamp and wait for the cement to cure, according to label instructions.<ref>http://www.jacobsrocketry.com/aer/casings_nozzles_headers.htm</ref>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Grind cat litter. Purchase unscented clay cat litter from a pet store. Keeping it dry, grind it in a coffee grinder or mortar until it becomes fine dust.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 3 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Pack the litter into each rocket. Stand each pipe on its end on a stable surface. Fill each one about ⅓ full with the cat litter dust. Tamp the litter vigorously with a wooden dowel or spool that fits snugly into the pipe. This should compress the litter into a hard, clay cap.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 4 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Purchase powdered sugar. Sugar provides the energy that will give thrust to the rocket once burned. Check the ingredients list before buying: most powdered sugar contains a little corn starch, but this will not significantly affect the rocket.<ref>http://www.nakka-rocketry.net/sucrose.html</ref> If it contains any other additives, look for a different brand.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 5 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find potassium nitrate. This chemical, KNO<sub>3</sub>, will provide oxygen to allow for fast, prolonged combustion. Buy this as \"stump killer\" at a garden supply store or home improvement store. Some brands of stump killer contain other ingredients, so check the label to make sure it is 100% KNO<sub>3</sub>.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 6 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Grind the potassium nitrate to a powder. Buy a new coffee grinder and label it \"potassium nitrate.\" Place it on a cleared counter, far away from all sugar and combustible materials. Fill halfway with potassium nitrate and grind for about 40 seconds, rotating the grinder to make sure all the powder hits the blades.<ref>http://www.nakka-rocketry.net/sucrose.html</ref> The finer the powder, the more evenly it will mix with the sugar.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 7 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find an appropriate work area. Once made, the rockets are at risk of combustion if they come into contact with heat, a spark from a metal object, or a flame. Ideally, you should make the rockets close to the area you plan to set them off. Choose an open area with no passersby. Even when intentionally set off, these rockets can damage surroundings or people when they return to earth.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 8 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Set up an electric heating station. Soon, you'll be mixing the two ingredients together over heat. There's always the risk of explosion or fire during this process. Reduce the chance of injury with the following setup:[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 9 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Wear safety equipment. There's a significant chance that the propellant mixture catches fire and explodes violently. Wear gloves, a face shield, and thick clothing that covers all exposed skin. Do not wear any clothing made from synthetic materials, which can melt onto your skin.",
                    "#Add ingredients to a heat-safe container. [[Use-a-Scale|Using a kitchen scale]], measure 65 grams (2.3 ounces) powdered potassium nitrate, and bring it to your heating station. Bring the kitchen scale to the powdered sugar. Measure 35 grams (1.2 ounces) in a new container and bring that to your station. Pour both ingredients into a saucepan or frying pan that you don't plan on using for any other purpose.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 11 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Mix with baking soda (optional). This will slow down the firing, which decreases thrust but reduces risk of exploding the rocket prematurely. Mix 15g (0.5 oz) baking soda into your 100g (3.5 oz) of propellant. Use a wooden or silicone implement to stir.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 12 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Heat while stirring frequently. Place the container of sugar and potassium nitrate on the electric heat source. Heat at 193ºC (380ºF), staying as near to this temperature as you can the whole time.<ref>http://www.nakka-rocketry.net/sucrose.html</ref> Using a silicone spatula (never a metal one), stir gently to combine the two ingredients and distribute the heat. Failure to stir frequently can cause an explosion. Stir over heat until the whole mixture turns into a thick, light brown paste, similar to peanut butter. This can take up to an hour, but a batch this size will usually be ready in 20–30 minutes.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 13 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Pack the propellant into the rocket body. As soon as the hot propellant mixture is ready, pour a little into one of your prepared rockets. Immediately tamp it down with a snug-fitting dowel, making sure there are no air bubbles. Pour more propellant and tamp down until there's roughly 2.5 cm (1 inch) of space left in the tube.<ref>ftp://ftp.demec.ufpr.br/foguete/bibliografia/ref_roc4_Easy_Pvc_Rockets_Smiley.pdf</ref>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 14 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Tamp down additional cat litter. Create a second clay cap on top of the propellant, just as you did the first. Tamp down firmly to create a hard, non-crumbling cap. This should extend beneath the retaining ring and lie even with the end of the rocket.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 15 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Drill carefully through the top cap. It's time to turn the cap you just made into a nozzle, which will create thrust by expelling high-pressure exhaust. It is possible to ignite the rocket by drilling, so use extreme caution. In your fire-safe work area, drill the nozzle as follows:[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 16 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Create a core (optional). After you've penetrated the cap, you may wish to create a hollow core in the propellant. This will increase thrust by providing more surface area to combust.<ref>http://exploration.grc.nasa.gov/education/rocket/TRCRocket/practical_rocketry.html</ref> Push a dowel or aluminum rod into the propellant, pushing about halfway along the rocket's length.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 17 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Attach a fuse. Thread a cannon fuse into the hole you just drilled. Leave plenty of fuse outside the rocket for safety.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 18 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Attach a stick to the side. Tape or glue a long, sturdy, wooden skewer to the outside of the rocket. Attach it near the nozzle, with most of the skewer extending beyond it.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 19 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Set in the ground and light. Plant the stick firmly into the ground so the rocket points upward. Confirm that the stick and rocket are stable. Warn everyone in the area. Light the fuse and step well back. Congratulations! You've launched your first sugar rocket.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 20 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ]
