        {
            "id": "17709386",
            "user": "Myles_Schaller",
            "timestamp": "22:02, 30 June 2015",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(14,155 bytes)",
            "change": 1,
            "summary": "(typo)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "cleanup",
            "reverted_by": "AtomicX",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Find potassium nitrate. This chemical, KNO<sub>3</sub>, will provide oxygen to allow for fast, prolonged combustion. Buy this as \"stump killer\" at a garden supply store or home improvement store. Some brands of stump killer contain other ingredients, so check the label to make sure it is 100% KNO<sub>3</sub>.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Find potassium nitrate. This chemical, KNO<sub>3</sub> will provide oxygen to allow for fast, prolonged combustion. Buy this as \"stump killer\" at a garden supply store or home improvement store. Some brands of stump killer contain other ingredients, so check the label to make sure it is 100% KNO<sub>3</sub>.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "17664442",
            "user": "Wikivisual",
            "timestamp": "02:06, 24 June 2015",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(14,154 bytes)",
            "change": 1111,
            "summary": "(Saving new step-by-step photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "reverted_by": "Snowflakeiesta",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Cut short sections of PVC piping. Buy roughly 13 mm (½ inch) diameter PVC piping from a home improvement store. [[Cut-PVC-Pipe|Cut the pipe]] into sections, as long as you'd like your rockets to be. 7.5–10 cm (3–4 in) rockets are a good place to start.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Add a retaining ring to each side. Find a smaller PVC pipe, that just barely fits into your rocket sections. Cut this into short sections, about 6–12 mm (¼–½ in) long. Cut through each section — this allows you to expand the ring outward for a tighter fit. Apply PVC cement to the inner edge of the larger pipe, at one end. Place the smaller pipe inside the larger one, pressing it outward to form a tight fit. Repeat on the opposite end with a second retaining ring. Clamp and wait for the cement to cure, according to label instructions.<ref>http://www.jacobsrocketry.com/aer/casings_nozzles_headers.htm</ref>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Grind cat litter. Purchase unscented clay cat litter from a pet store. Keeping it dry, grind it in a coffee grinder or mortar until it becomes fine dust.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Pack the litter into each rocket. Stand each pipe on its end on a stable surface. Fill each one about ⅓ full with the cat litter dust. Tamp the litter vigorously with a wooden dowel or spool that fits snugly into the pipe. This should compress the litter into a hard, clay cap.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Purchase powdered sugar. Sugar provides the energy that will give thrust to the rocket once burned. Check the ingredients list before buying: most powdered sugar contains a little corn starch, but this will not significantly affect the rocket.<ref>http://www.nakka-rocketry.net/sucrose.html</ref> If it contains any other additives, look for a different brand.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find potassium nitrate. This chemical, KNO<sub>3</sub> will provide oxygen to allow for fast, prolonged combustion. Buy this as \"stump killer\" at a garden supply store or home improvement store. Some brands of stump killer contain other ingredients, so check the label to make sure it is 100% KNO<sub>3</sub>.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Grind the potassium nitrate to a powder. Buy a new coffee grinder and label it \"potassium nitrate.\" Place it on a cleared counter, far away from all sugar and combustible materials. Fill halfway with potassium nitrate and grind for about 40 seconds, rotating the grinder to make sure all the powder hits the blades.<ref>http://www.nakka-rocketry.net/sucrose.html</ref> The finer the powder, the more evenly it will mix with the sugar.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find an appropriate work area. Once made, the rockets are at risk of combustion if they come into contact with heat, a spark from a metal object, or a flame. Ideally, you should make the rockets close to the area you plan to set them off. Choose an open area with no passersby. Even when intentionally set off, these rockets can damage surroundings or people when they return to earth.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 8 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Set up an electric heating station. Soon, you'll be mixing the two ingredients together over heat. There's always the risk of explosion or fire during this process. Reduce the chance of injury with the following setup:[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Wear safety equipment. There's a significant chance that the propellant mixture catches fire and explodes violently. Wear gloves, a face shield, and thick clothing that covers all exposed skin. Do not wear any clothing made from synthetic materials, which can melt onto your skin.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 10 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Add ingredients to a heat-safe container. [[Use-a-Scale|Using a kitchen scale]], measure 65 grams (2.3 ounces) powdered potassium nitrate, and bring it to your heating station. Bring the kitchen scale to the powdered sugar. Measure 35 grams (1.2 ounces) in a new container and bring that to your station. Pour both ingredients into a saucepan or frying pan that you don't plan on using for any other purpose.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 11 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Mix with baking soda (optional). This will slow down the firing, which decreases thrust but reduces risk of exploding the rocket prematurely. Mix 15g (0.5 oz) baking soda into your 100g (3.5 oz) of propellant. Use a wooden or silicone implement to stir.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 12 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Heat while stirring frequently. Place the container of sugar and potassium nitrate on the electric heat source. Heat at 193ºC (380ºF), staying as near to this temperature as you can the whole time.<ref>http://www.nakka-rocketry.net/sucrose.html</ref> Using a silicone spatula (never a metal one), stir gently to combine the two ingredients and distribute the heat. Failure to stir frequently can cause an explosion. Stir over heat until the whole mixture turns into a thick, light brown paste, similar to peanut butter. This can take up to an hour, but a batch this size will usually be ready in 20–30 minutes.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 13 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Pack the propellant into the rocket body. As soon as the hot propellant mixture is ready, pour a little into one of your prepared rockets. Immediately tamp it down with a snug-fitting dowel, making sure there are no air bubbles. Pour more propellant and tamp down until there's roughly 2.5 cm (1 inch) of space left in the tube.<ref>ftp://ftp.demec.ufpr.br/foguete/bibliografia/ref_roc4_Easy_Pvc_Rockets_Smiley.pdf</ref>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 14 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Tamp down additional cat litter. Create a second clay cap on top of the propellant, just as you did the first. Tamp down firmly to create a hard, non-crumbling cap. This should extend beneath the retaining ring and lie even with the end of the rocket.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 15 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Drill carefully through the top cap. It's time to turn the cap you just made into a nozzle, which will create thrust by expelling high-pressure exhaust. It is possible to ignite the rocket by drilling, so use extreme caution. In your fire-safe work area, drill the nozzle as follows:[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 16 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Create a core (optional). After you've penetrated the cap, you may wish to create a hollow core in the propellant. This will increase thrust by providing more surface area to combust.<ref>http://exploration.grc.nasa.gov/education/rocket/TRCRocket/practical_rocketry.html</ref> Push a dowel or aluminum rod into the propellant, pushing about halfway along the rocket's length.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 17 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Attach a fuse. Thread a cannon fuse into the hole you just drilled. Leave plenty of fuse outside the rocket for safety.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 18 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Attach a stick to the side. Tape or glue a long, sturdy, wooden skewer to the outside of the rocket. Attach it near the nozzle, with most of the skewer extending beyond it.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 19 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Set in the ground and light. Plant the stick firmly into the ground so the rocket points upward. Confirm that the stick and rocket are stable. Warn everyone in the area. Light the fuse and step well back. Congratulations! You've launched your first sugar rocket.[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 20.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Cut short sections of PVC piping. Buy roughly 13 mm (½ inch) diameter PVC piping from a home improvement store. [[Cut-PVC-Pipe|Cut the pipe]] into sections, as long as you'd like your rockets to be. 7.5–10 cm (3–4 in) rockets are a good place to start.",
                    "#Add a retaining ring to each side. Find a smaller PVC pipe, that just barely fits into your rocket sections. Cut this into short sections, about 6–12 mm (¼–½ in) long. Cut through each section — this allows you to expand the ring outward for a tighter fit. Apply PVC cement to the inner edge of the larger pipe, at one end. Place the smaller pipe inside the larger one, pressing it outward to form a tight fit. Repeat on the opposite end with a second retaining ring. Clamp and wait for the cement to cure, according to label instructions.<ref>http://www.jacobsrocketry.com/aer/casings_nozzles_headers.htm</ref>",
                    "#Grind cat litter. Purchase unscented clay cat litter from a pet store. Keeping it dry, grind it in a coffee grinder or mortar until it becomes fine dust.",
                    "#Pack the litter into each rocket. Stand each pipe on its end on a stable surface. Fill each one about ⅓ full with the cat litter dust. Tamp the litter vigorously with a wooden dowel or spool that fits snugly into the pipe. This should compress the litter into a hard, clay cap.",
                    "#Purchase powdered sugar. Sugar provides the energy that will give thrust to the rocket once burned. Check the ingredients list before buying: most powdered sugar contains a little corn starch, but this will not significantly affect the rocket.<ref>http://www.nakka-rocketry.net/sucrose.html</ref> If it contains any other additives, look for a different brand.",
                    "#Find potassium nitrate. This chemical, KNO<sub>3</sub> will provide oxygen to allow for fast, prolonged combustion. Buy this as \"stump killer\" at a garden supply store or home improvement store. Some brands of stump killer contain other ingredients, so check the label to make sure it is 100% KNO<sub>3</sub>.",
                    "#Grind the potassium nitrate to a powder. Buy a new coffee grinder and label it \"potassium nitrate.\" Place it on a cleared counter, far away from all sugar and combustible materials. Fill halfway with potassium nitrate and grind for about 40 seconds, rotating the grinder to make sure all the powder hits the blades.<ref>http://www.nakka-rocketry.net/sucrose.html</ref> The finer the powder, the more evenly it will mix with the sugar.",
                    "#Find an appropriate work area. Once made, the rockets are at risk of combustion if they come into contact with heat, a spark from a metal object, or a flame. Ideally, you should make the rockets close to the area you plan to set them off. Choose an open area with no passersby. Even when intentionally set off, these rockets can damage surroundings or people when they return to earth.",
                    "#Set up an electric heating station. Soon, you'll be mixing the two ingredients together over heat. There's always the risk of explosion or fire during this process. Reduce the chance of injury with the following setup:",
                    "#Wear safety equipment. There's a significant chance that the propellant mixture catches fire and explodes violently. Wear gloves, a face shield, and thick clothing that covers all exposed skin. Do not wear any clothing made from synthetic materials, which can melt onto your skin.",
                    "#Add ingredients to a heat-safe container. [[Use-a-Scale|Using a kitchen scale]], measure 65 grams (2.3 ounces) powdered potassium nitrate, and bring it to your heating station. Bring the kitchen scale to the powdered sugar. Measure 35 grams (1.2 ounces) in a new container and bring that to your station. Pour both ingredients into a saucepan or frying pan that you don't plan on using for any other purpose.",
                    "#Mix with baking soda (optional). This will slow down the firing, which decreases thrust but reduces risk of exploding the rocket prematurely. Mix 15g (0.5 oz) baking soda into your 100g (3.5 oz) of propellant. Use a wooden or silicone implement to stir.",
                    "#Heat while stirring frequently. Place the container of sugar and potassium nitrate on the electric heat source. Heat at 193ºC (380ºF), staying as near to this temperature as you can the whole time.<ref>http://www.nakka-rocketry.net/sucrose.html</ref> Using a silicone spatula (never a metal one), stir gently to combine the two ingredients and distribute the heat. Failure to stir frequently can cause an explosion. Stir over heat until the whole mixture turns into a thick, light brown paste, similar to peanut butter. This can take up to an hour, but a batch this size will usually be ready in 20–30 minutes.",
                    "#Pack the propellant into the rocket body. As soon as the hot propellant mixture is ready, pour a little into one of your prepared rockets. Immediately tamp it down with a snug-fitting dowel, making sure there are no air bubbles. Pour more propellant and tamp down until there's roughly 2.5 cm (1 inch) of space left in the tube.<ref>ftp://ftp.demec.ufpr.br/foguete/bibliografia/ref_roc4_Easy_Pvc_Rockets_Smiley.pdf</ref>",
                    "#Tamp down additional cat litter. Create a second clay cap on top of the propellant, just as you did the first. Tamp down firmly to create a hard, non-crumbling cap. This should extend beneath the retaining ring and lie even with the end of the rocket.",
                    "#Drill carefully through the top cap. It's time to turn the cap you just made into a nozzle, which will create thrust by expelling high-pressure exhaust. It is possible to ignite the rocket by drilling, so use extreme caution. In your fire-safe work area, drill the nozzle as follows:",
                    "#Create a core (optional). After you've penetrated the cap, you may wish to create a hollow core in the propellant. This will increase thrust by providing more surface area to combust.<ref>http://exploration.grc.nasa.gov/education/rocket/TRCRocket/practical_rocketry.html</ref> Push a dowel or aluminum rod into the propellant, pushing about halfway along the rocket's length.",
                    "#Attach a fuse. Thread a cannon fuse into the hole you just drilled. Leave plenty of fuse outside the rocket for safety.",
                    "#Attach a stick to the side. Tape or glue a long, sturdy, wooden skewer to the outside of the rocket. Attach it near the nozzle, with most of the skewer extending beyond it.",
                    "#Set in the ground and light. Plant the stick firmly into the ground so the rocket points upward. Confirm that the stick and rocket are stable. Warn everyone in the area. Light the fuse and step well back. Congratulations! You've launched your first sugar rocket."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "17513285",
            "user": "Myles_Schaller",
            "timestamp": "05:55, 3 June 2015",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(13,043 bytes)",
            "change": 5292,
            "summary": "(Rewrite with more effective and safer method, + more detail)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "reverted_by": "AtomicX",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Sugar rockets sound innocuous, but they produce thrust that can lift them hundreds of meters in the air. Before you begin, make sure you have an open, uninhabited area where you can make or launch them. Read through the whole process before you get started, so you are prepared to follow all safety precautions.",
                    "",
                    "===Making the Rocket Body===",
                    "#Cut short sections of PVC piping. Buy roughly 13 mm (½ inch) diameter PVC piping from a home improvement store. [[Cut-PVC-Pipe|Cut the pipe]] into sections, as long as you'd like your rockets to be. 7.5–10 cm (3–4 in) rockets are a good place to start.",
                    "#*Do not replace this with a metal pipe. Sparks from the metal could ignite your rocket and cause a premature explosion.",
                    "#Add a retaining ring to each side. Find a smaller PVC pipe, that just barely fits into your rocket sections. Cut this into short sections, about 6–12 mm (¼–½ in) long. Cut through each section — this allows you to expand the ring outward for a tighter fit. Apply PVC cement to the inner edge of the larger pipe, at one end. Place the smaller pipe inside the larger one, pressing it outward to form a tight fit. Repeat on the opposite end with a second retaining ring. Clamp and wait for the cement to cure, according to label instructions.<ref>http://www.jacobsrocketry.com/aer/casings_nozzles_headers.htm</ref>",
                    "#Grind cat litter. Purchase unscented clay cat litter from a pet store. Keeping it dry, grind it in a coffee grinder or mortar until it becomes fine dust.",
                    "#*Alternatively, use quick-set cement.",
                    "#*Eventually, you'll be making a cap on the opposite end as well. Grind extra and set it aside.",
                    "#Pack the litter into each rocket. Stand each pipe on its end on a stable surface. Fill each one about ⅓ full with the cat litter dust. Tamp the litter vigorously with a wooden dowel or spool that fits snugly into the pipe. This should compress the litter into a hard, clay cap.",
                    "#*Make sure the clay forms a hard surface above the retaining ring. The ring's job is to prevent the clay from sliding out, allowing the rocket to build up more pressure before the cap blows out.",
                    "#*If the litter crumbles and refuses to pack, dampen it slightly.",
                    "===Making the Propellant===",
                    "#Purchase powdered sugar. Sugar provides the energy that will give thrust to the rocket once burned. Check the ingredients list before buying: most powdered sugar contains a little corn starch, but this will not significantly affect the rocket.<ref>http://www.nakka-rocketry.net/sucrose.html</ref> If it contains any other additives, look for a different brand.",
                    "#*In some areas, this is sold as confectioner's sugar or icing sugar.",
                    "#*You can start with granulated white sugar and [[Make-Powdered-Sugar|turn it into powdered sugar]] with a blender, coffee grinder, or spice mill.",
                    "#Find potassium nitrate. This chemical, KNO<sub>3</sub> will provide oxygen to allow for fast, prolonged combustion. Buy this as \"stump killer\" at a garden supply store or home improvement store. Some brands of stump killer contain other ingredients, so check the label to make sure it is 100% KNO<sub>3</sub>.",
                    "#*You can sometimes find potassium nitrate at pharmacies, farming supply stores, or online chemistry supply stores. For best results, look for it in powdered form.",
                    "#*Keep the potassium nitrate and the sugar in separate rooms.",
                    "#Grind the potassium nitrate to a powder. Buy a new coffee grinder and label it \"potassium nitrate.\" Place it on a cleared counter, far away from all sugar and combustible materials. Fill halfway with potassium nitrate and grind for about 40 seconds, rotating the grinder to make sure all the powder hits the blades.<ref>http://www.nakka-rocketry.net/sucrose.html</ref> The finer the powder, the more evenly it will mix with the sugar.",
                    "#*Never grind sugar and potassium nitrate in the same grinder, even in separate batches. This can cause a fire or explosion.",
                    "#*You'll need 65g (2.3 oz) for this batch, about a handful.",
                    "#Find an appropriate work area. Once made, the rockets are at risk of combustion if they come into contact with heat, a spark from a metal object, or a flame. Ideally, you should make the rockets close to the area you plan to set them off. Choose an open area with no passersby. Even when intentionally set off, these rockets can damage surroundings or people when they return to earth.",
                    "#*Check local law for regulations concerning rockets or fireworks.",
                    "#Set up an electric heating station. Soon, you'll be mixing the two ingredients together over heat. There's always the risk of explosion or fire during this process. Reduce the chance of injury with the following setup:",
                    "#*Remove all clutter and flammable materials from a spacious area, preferably outside. The floor should be made of dirt (with all weeds removed) or concrete.",
                    "#*Set up an electric hot plate or deep fryer with a thermostat control. An electric stove or other heating element without an exact temperature setting adds significant danger.<ref>http://www.nakka-rocketry.net/sucrose.html</ref>",
                    "#*Make sure there are no sources of sparks or open flame in the area. Even metal objects can be dangerous.",
                    "#*Keep a large container of water nearby. A fire extinguisher may not extinguish burning propellant.<ref>http://www.nakka-rocketry.net/sucrose.html</ref>",
                    "#Wear safety equipment. There's a significant chance that the propellant mixture catches fire and explodes violently. Wear gloves, a face shield, and thick clothing that covers all exposed skin. Do not wear any clothing made from synthetic materials, which can melt onto your skin.",
                    "#*Use a face shield that protects your head and hair as well.",
                    "#*A leather apron and long leather gloves are recommended.",
                    "#Add ingredients to a heat-safe container. [[Use-a-Scale|Using a kitchen scale]], measure 65 grams (2.3 ounces) powdered potassium nitrate, and bring it to your heating station. Bring the kitchen scale to the powdered sugar. Measure 35 grams (1.2 ounces) in a new container and bring that to your station. Pour both ingredients into a saucepan or frying pan that you don't plan on using for any other purpose.",
                    "#*For best results, make a double boiler by placing a deep saucepan into a larger pan filled with oil. This heats the propellant more evenly.<ref>http://www.jacobsrocketry.com/aer/caramel_candy_propellant.htm</ref>",
                    "#*For your first project, consider using 60g (2.1 oz) potassium nitrate and 40g (1.4 oz) sugar instead. This is much easier to make, but less powerful.<ref>http://www.nakka-rocketry.net/sucrose.html</ref>",
                    "#Mix with baking soda (optional). This will slow down the firing, which decreases thrust but reduces risk of exploding the rocket prematurely. Mix 15g (0.5 oz) baking soda into your 100g (3.5 oz) of propellant. Use a wooden or silicone implement to stir.",
                    "#Heat while stirring frequently. Place the container of sugar and potassium nitrate on the electric heat source. Heat at 193ºC (380ºF), staying as near to this temperature as you can the whole time.<ref>http://www.nakka-rocketry.net/sucrose.html</ref> Using a silicone spatula (never a metal one), stir gently to combine the two ingredients and distribute the heat. Failure to stir frequently can cause an explosion. Stir over heat until the whole mixture turns into a thick, light brown paste, similar to peanut butter. This can take up to an hour, but a batch this size will usually be ready in 20–30 minutes.",
                    "#*If the sugar turns dark brown, remove the mixture from heat. Too much caramelization will make the propellant less effective.",
                    "",
                    "===Finishing the Rocket===",
                    "#Pack the propellant into the rocket body. As soon as the hot propellant mixture is ready, pour a little into one of your prepared rockets. Immediately tamp it down with a snug-fitting dowel, making sure there are no air bubbles. Pour more propellant and tamp down until there's roughly 2.5 cm (1 inch) of space left in the tube.<ref>ftp://ftp.demec.ufpr.br/foguete/bibliografia/ref_roc4_Easy_Pvc_Rockets_Smiley.pdf</ref>",
                    "#*If the mixture has cooled too much to pour, transfer the material with a wooden implement.",
                    "#*Leave space between the propellant and the retaining ring.",
                    "#Tamp down additional cat litter. Create a second clay cap on top of the propellant, just as you did the first. Tamp down firmly to create a hard, non-crumbling cap. This should extend beneath the retaining ring and lie even with the end of the rocket.",
                    "#*Again, you may use quick-set cement instead. Allow cement to cure before you continue.",
                    "#*At this point, the rocket will move with extreme force if the propellant ignites. Stand back while tamping. From now on, treat the rocket with extreme caution and avoid pointing either end toward yourself.",
                    "#Drill carefully through the top cap. It's time to turn the cap you just made into a nozzle, which will create thrust by expelling high-pressure exhaust. It is possible to ignite the rocket by drilling, so use extreme caution. In your fire-safe work area, drill the nozzle as follows:",
                    "#*Clamp your rocket in place and stand to one side. Never put your face in front of the rocket end.",
                    "#*Choose a small drill bit, to make a small hole in the center of the rocket. A smaller hole leads to higher pressure, but this may blow off the cap prematurely. You may need to experiment to find the best results.",
                    "#*Use the slowest drill setting to keep temperatures low. Drill through the center of the clay cap. Stop every few seconds and withdraw to reduce heat and wipe off stuck particles with a dry cloth.",
                    "#*Drill until you penetrate the top cap.",
                    "#Create a core (optional). After you've penetrated the cap, you may wish to create a hollow core in the propellant. This will increase thrust by providing more surface area to combust.<ref>http://exploration.grc.nasa.gov/education/rocket/TRCRocket/practical_rocketry.html</ref> Push a dowel or aluminum rod into the propellant, pushing about halfway along the rocket's length.",
                    "#*Your propellant may be too viscous or too hard to create the core. That's fine; the rocket will still work.",
                    "#*Remember, do not put your face in front of either end of the rocket.",
                    "#Attach a fuse. Thread a cannon fuse into the hole you just drilled. Leave plenty of fuse outside the rocket for safety.",
                    "#Attach a stick to the side. Tape or glue a long, sturdy, wooden skewer to the outside of the rocket. Attach it near the nozzle, with most of the skewer extending beyond it.",
                    "#*You should be able to balance the rocket (just above the ground) on a finger placed just above the nozzle. Move the stick or switch to a stick of a different size until you can balance it.<ref>ftp://ftp.demec.ufpr.br/foguete/bibliografia/ref_roc4_Easy_Pvc_Rockets_Smiley.pdf</ref>",
                    "#Set in the ground and light. Plant the stick firmly into the ground so the rocket points upward. Confirm that the stick and rocket are stable. Warn everyone in the area. Light the fuse and step well back. Congratulations! You've launched your first sugar rocket.",
                    "#*Preferably, move behind a safety wall after lighting the fuse.",
                    "*Store all ingredients and propellant in an airtight container to minimize the amount of moisture they absorb from the air. (To reduce fire risk, only store propellant for a day or two, while completing the project.)",
                    "*If your stump remover is not 100% KNO<sub>3</sub>, dissolve it in boiling water and strain it through a paper filter. Discard the filter and solids, then boil off remaining water to get mostly pure KNO<sub>3</sub>. Leave in a hot area or minimum-temperature oven until completely dry.<ref>ftp://ftp.demec.ufpr.br/foguete/bibliografia/ref_roc4_Easy_Pvc_Rockets_Smiley.pdf</ref>",
                    "*For an extremely fine powder with excellent combustive qualities, put the sugar and the nitrate (always separated) into individual rock tumblers. Tumble for up to 10 hours.",
                    "*This is a highly dangerous process that should be treated with caution. Older children should not try this without constant, close supervision. Younger children should not be kept near the area.",
                    "*Check local laws before making or firing rockets. They may be considered fireworks or weapons in some areas.",
                    "*If anyone else has access to your workspace, post visible warning signs at all entrances.",
                    "*PVC pipe, about 13 mm (½ inch) diameter",
                    "*PVC pipe, about 6 mm (¼ inch) diameter (tight fit in the larger pipe)",
                    "*PVC cement",
                    "*Clay cat litter ''or'' quick-set cement",
                    "*Wooden dowel or spool (tight fit in the larger pipe)",
                    "*Potassium nitrate (KNO<sub>3</sub>)",
                    "*Electric deep fryer or hot plate with thermostat control",
                    "*Face shield",
                    "*Gloves and thick clothing, non-synthetic (preferably flame-resistant)",
                    "*Drill",
                    "*Vice or clamp",
                    "*Kebab stick.",
                    "",
                    "==Sources and Citations==",
                    "{{reflist}}",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "__PARTS__"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "How to make homemade core burning rockets out of powdered sugar, potassium nitrate, water putty, paper and a core burning rocket spindle.",
                    "Don't use any steel when making rockets because of the spark hazard.  Test fire rockets on launching stands where there are no weeds or flammable materials nearby.  It is best to conduct rocket tests after it just rained. That's what i do because I live in a desert and everything is flammable.",
                    "=== Propellant ===",
                    "#Get a good propellant to lift the rocket.  The size of the rocket will determine whether you need a slow or fast burning propellant.  For small rockets, the faster the burn the better, while  larger rockets need a slower burning propellant so the rocket does not exceed pressure limits and explode.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Gather your propellant materials.",
                    "#*You'll need KNO<sub>3</sub> (Potassium nitrate) to act as an oxidizer for your fuel. You can buy this at any Home Depot/Lowe's as stump remover (the brands Spectracide and Grant's are both pure KNO<sub>3</sub>; others may be as well<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 2Bullet1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*Buy powdered sugar.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 2Bullet2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*Get a ball mill with Lead balls.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 2Bullet3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Dig a hole if you don't have sandbags to put the tumbler in because of the de-fragmentation hazard of the propellant being mixed.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Boil 2 cups water and add 2 pounds KNO<sub>3</sub> until all of it is dissolved.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Boil out all the water until you get a crusty, white powder left.  That was fractional recrystallization; the KNO<sub>3</sub> was piled, so we dissolved it in water to break the strong crystal formations and get a powder.  It should be clumpy and look like a cake, so get a hammer and break the clumps and throw it in the rock tumbler and tumble it until it's a very fine dust.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 5.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Add the powdered sugar, by weight rather than volume.  You should have 60%-65% KNO<sub>3</sub> 40%-35% Sugar (optional 5% Mg dust for nice tail, also adding 1% Red Iron Oxide for faster burn rate).<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 6.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Add KNO<sub>3</sub> and sugar to the rock tumbler with marbles and place it in a hole or pile sandbags around it.  Propellants are not explosive, but they do burn fast.  A fast burn could be 4000fps yet it would still be a fast burn. When a substance detonates, the entire substance turns to a gas.  This is much quicker, more efficient, and the reaction is hotter, so it produces thousands of atoms of pressure, compared to propellants that could produce half that under ideal conditions.  You will get a finished product in 6 hours of tumbling.  Be extremely careful when turning on and off the tumbler because it could burst if the powder takes fire.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 7.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Test your propellant by putting 1/8 of a teaspoon of propellant on a board outside and carefully lighting it. You should get a fast burn and little residue of carbon on the test surface.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 8.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Continue to make many different mixes if the propellant does not burn fast enough.  The entire pile should go up in a puff of smoke like a ninja smoke bomb; don't make it any faster!  Don't leave the rocket tumbler on for the entire weekend or it will become very sensitive to shock.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 9.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "=== Rocket Body ===",
                    "#Case your propellant in a wrapped up piece of paper.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 10.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Wrap the paper in layers while putting glue around the ram rod up to 1/8 of an inch thick and let it dry overnight.  Making the motor casing is harder to make then the propellant.  Make sure there are no bubbles in the casing or during the burn, the pressure with burn a hole in the motor.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 11.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Mix the water putty until it is very thick and not sticky.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 12.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Put the water putty on the spindle.  A little will go a long way, so don't overdo it.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 13.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Put the motor casing in the spindle with the putty at the bast.  With a rubber mallet, give the putty light taps until it has taken the form of the nozzle. Remove it slowly from the spindle and let the putty dry for an hour.  Put the motor casing back on the spindle and charge with propellant.  Pound until it is very stiff.  The higher the density of the propellant in the rocket, the more thrust it will have.  Continue to add propellant until it is clear of the spindle and add the delay propellant.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 14.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "=== Delay propellant ===",
                    "#<br>This is normal propellant with 10% Baking soda to decrease thrust.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 15.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Add an end plug made of water putty.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 16.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Let the rocket dry on a rack and in an airtight container because the propellant is hygroscopic, which means it will take out the water from the air and add it to the propellant, making it a poor rocket.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 17.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Buy FFF gunpowder and coat the core of the rocket so it takes fire easily.  Insert a visco fuse, also known as a cannon fuse, into the nozzle.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 18.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Glue the guidance stick to the side of the rocket.  Make sure the rocket will be stable in flight by placing the rocket on your finger just behind the nozzle.  If it balances or the stick side falls, then it will be stable.",
                    "#Balance your rocket with clay and just have fun because it is only the beginning of what you can do!<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 19.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "== Video ==",
                    "{{Video:Make Sugar Rockets|}}",
                    "*Keep modifying the propellant core length and amount of KNO<sub>3</sub> to carbon.",
                    "*It takes practice so don't get discouraged if your first rocket does not fly.",
                    "*In order to get the best thrust out of your rockets make sure you prime your rockets with FFF gunpowder.",
                    "*Check local laws.",
                    "*A safer method to test the rocket propellant is to get a jar, add sugar and KNO<sub>3</sub> to the correct amount and shake it for as long as possible.  The propellant will be poor and low impulse, so do what you think is right.",
                    "*To launch correctly, all the propellant must take fire, so if you don't prime the rocket, it won't fly right, or won't even get off the pad.",
                    "*Don't go to any chemical supply stores for potassium nitrate!",
                    "*Rockets are not toys.",
                    "*This is for reference only!",
                    "*Your launch site may go up in flames; you need to be prepared to fight a fire.",
                    "*Use a ball mill with lead media to reduce the oxidizer (kno3) and (sugar)   separately in order to eliminate any deflagration hazard.",
                    "*KNO3 or potassium nitrate",
                    "*heavy crafting paper",
                    "*Bottle rocket kit",
                    "*Water putty",
                    "*Guide stick",
                    "*Rock tumbler",
                    "*Lead balls or media",
                    "*Sandbags",
                    "*Extension cord",
                    "*Ball mill"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
