                "added": [
                    "#Get a good propellant to lift the rocket.  The size of the rocket will determine whether you need a slow or fast burning propellant.  For small rockets, the faster the burn the better, while  larger rockets need a slower burning propellant so the rocket does not exceed pressure limits and burst.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*You'll need KNO<sub>3</sub> (Potassium nitrate) to act as an oxidizer for your fuel. You can buy this at any Home Depot/Lowe's as stump remover (the brands Spectracide and Grant's are both pure KNO<sub>3</sub>; others may be as well<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 2Bullet1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*Buy powdered sugar.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 2Bullet2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*Get a ball mill with Lead balls.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 2Bullet3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Dig a hole if you don't have sandbags to put the tumbler in because of the de-fragmentation hazard of the propellant being mixed.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Boil 2 cups water and add 2 pounds KNO<sub>3</sub> until all of it is dissolved.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Boil out all the water until you get a crusty, white powder left.  That was fractional recrystallization; the KNO<sub>3</sub> was piled, so we dissolved it in water to break the strong crystal formations and get a powder.  It should be clumpy and look like a cake, so get a hammer and break the clumps and throw it in the rock tumbler and tumble it until it's a very fine dust.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 5.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Add the powdered sugar, by weight rather than volume.  You should have 60%-65% KNO<sub>3</sub> 40%-35% Sugar (optional 5% Mg dust for nice tail, also adding 1% Red Iron Oxide for faster burn rate).<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 6.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Add KNO<sub>3</sub> and sugar to the rock tumbler with marbles and place it in a hole or pile sand bags around it.  Propellants are not explosive, but they do burn fast.  A fast burn could be 4000fps yet it would still be a fast burn. When a substance detonates, the entire substance turns to a gas.  This is much quicker, more efficient, and the reaction is hotter, so it produces thousands of atoms of pressure, compared to propellants that could produce half that under ideal conditions.  You will get a finished product in 6 hours of tumbling.  Be extremely careful when turning on and off the tumbler because it could burst if the powder takes fire.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 7.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Test your propellant by putting 1/8 of a teaspoon of propellant on a board outside and and carefully lighting it. You should get a fast burn and little residue of carbon on the test surface.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 8.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Continue to make many different mixes if the propellant if it does not burn fast enough.  The entire pile should go up in a puff of smoke like a ninja smoke bomb; don't make it any faster!  Don't leave the rocket tumbler on for the entire weekend or it will become very sensitive to shock.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 9.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Case your propellant in a wrapped up piece of paper.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 10.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Wrap the paper in layers while putting glue around the ram rod up to 1/8 of an inch thick and let it dry over night.  Making the motor casing is harder to make then the propellant.  Make sure there are no bubbles in the casing or during the burn, the pressure with burn a hole in the motor.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 11.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Mix the water putty until it is very thick and not sticky.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 12.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Put the water putty on the spindle.  A little will go a long way, so don't overdo it.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 13.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Put the motor casing in the spindle with the putty at the bast.  With a rubber mallet, give the putty light taps until it has taken the form of the nozzle. Remove it slowly from the spindle and let the putty dry for an hour.  Put the motor casing back on the spindle and charge with propellant.  Pound until it is very stiff.  The higher the density of the propellant in the rocket, the more thrust it will have.  Continue to add propellant until it is clear of the spindle and add the delay propellant.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 14.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#<br>This is normal propellant with 10% Baking soda to decrease thrust.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 15.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Add an end plug made of water putty.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 16.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Let the rocket dry on a rack and in an air tight container because the propellant is hydroscopic, which means it will take out the water from the air and add it to the propellant, making it a poor rocket.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 17.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Buy FFF gun powder and coat the core of the rocket so it takes fire easily.  Insert a visco fuse, also known as a cannon fuse, into the nozzle.<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 18.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Glue the guidance stick to the side of the rocket.  Make sure the rocket will be stable in flight by placing the rocket on your finger just behind the nozzle.  If it balances or the stick side falls, then it will be stable. #Balance your rocket with clay and just have fun because it is only the beginning of what you can do!<br><br>[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 19.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    ""
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 1.jpg|right|300px]]Get a good propellant to lift the rocket.  The size of the rocket will determine whether you need a slow or fast burning propellant.  For small rockets, the faster the burn the better, while  larger rockets need a slower burning propellant so the rocket does not exceed pressure limits and burst.",
                    "#*[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 2Bullet1.jpg|right|300px]]You'll need KNO<sub>3</sub> (Potassium nitrate) to act as an oxidizer for your fuel. You can buy this at any Home Depot/Lowe's as stump remover (the brands Spectracide and Grant's are both pure KNO<sub>3</sub>; others may be as well",
                    "#*[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 2Bullet2.jpg|right|300px]]Buy powdered sugar.",
                    "#*[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 2Bullet3.jpg|right|300px]]Get a ball mill with Lead balls.",
                    "#[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 3.jpg|right|300px]]Dig a hole if you don't have sandbags to put the tumbler in because of the de-fragmentation hazard of the propellant being mixed.",
                    "#[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 4.jpg|right|300px]]Boil 2 cups water and add 2 pounds KNO<sub>3</sub> until all of it is dissolved.",
                    "#[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 5.jpg|right|300px]]Boil out all the water until you get a crusty, white powder left.  That was fractional recrystallization; the KNO<sub>3</sub> was piled, so we dissolved it in water to break the strong crystal formations and get a powder.  It should be clumpy and look like a cake, so get a hammer and break the clumps and throw it in the rock tumbler and tumble it until it's a very fine dust.",
                    "#[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 6.jpg|right|300px]]Add the powdered sugar, by weight rather than volume.  You should have 60%-65% KNO<sub>3</sub> 40%-35% Sugar (optional 5% Mg dust for nice tail, also adding 1% Red Iron Oxide for faster burn rate).",
                    "#[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 7.jpg|right|300px]]Add KNO<sub>3</sub> and sugar to the rock tumbler with marbles and place it in a hole or pile sand bags around it.  Propellants are not explosive, but they do burn fast.  A fast burn could be 4000fps yet it would still be a fast burn. When a substance detonates, the entire substance turns to a gas.  This is much quicker, more efficient, and the reaction is hotter, so it produces thousands of atoms of pressure, compared to propellants that could produce half that under ideal conditions.  You will get a finished product in 6 hours of tumbling.  Be extremely careful when turning on and off the tumbler because it could burst if the powder takes fire.",
                    "#[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 8.jpg|right|300px]]Test your propellant by putting 1/8 of a teaspoon of propellant on a board outside and and carefully lighting it. You should get a fast burn and little residue of carbon on the test surface.",
                    "#[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 9.jpg|right|300px]]Continue to make many different mixes if the propellant if it does not burn fast enough.  The entire pile should go up in a puff of smoke like a ninja smoke bomb; don't make it any faster!  Don't leave the rocket tumbler on for the entire weekend or it will become very sensitive to shock.",
                    "#[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 10.jpg|right|300px]]Case your propellant in a wrapped up piece of paper.",
                    "#[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 11.jpg|right|300px]]Wrap the paper in layers while putting glue around the ram rod up to 1/8 of an inch thick and let it dry over night.  Making the motor casing is harder to make then the propellant.  Make sure there are no bubbles in the casing or during the burn, the pressure with burn a hole in the motor.",
                    "#[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 12.jpg|right|300px]]Mix the water putty until it is very thick and not sticky.",
                    "#[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 13.jpg|right|300px]]Put the water putty on the spindle.  A little will go a long way, so don't overdo it.",
                    "#[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 14.jpg|right|300px]]Put the motor casing in the spindle with the putty at the bast.  With a rubber mallet, give the putty light taps until it has taken the form of the nozzle. Remove it slowly from the spindle and let the putty dry for an hour.  Put the motor casing back on the spindle and charge with propellant.  Pound until it is very stiff.  The higher the density of the propellant in the rocket, the more thrust it will have.  Continue to add propellant until it is clear of the spindle and add the delay propellant.",
                    "#[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 15.jpg|right|300px]]<br>This is normal propellant with 10% Baking soda to decrease thrust.",
                    "#[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 16.jpg|right|300px]]Add an end plug made of water putty.",
                    "#[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 17.jpg|right|300px]]Let the rocket dry on a rack and in an air tight container because the propellant is hydroscopic, which means it will take out the water from the air and add it to the propellant, making it a poor rocket.",
                    "#[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 18.jpg|right|300px]]Buy FFF gun powder and coat the core of the rocket so it takes fire easily.  Insert a visco fuse, also known as a cannon fuse, into the nozzle.",
                    "#[[Image:Make Sugar Rockets Step 19.jpg|right|300px]]Glue the guidance stick to the side of the rocket.  Make sure the rocket will be stable in flight by placing the rocket on your finger just behind the nozzle.  If it balances or the stick side falls, then it will be stable. #Balance your rocket with clay and just have fun because it is only the beginning of what you can do!"
                ]
            }
        },
